UPDATE Beats SET [Text]=N'Wen had been waiting at the base of the freight ramp for the better part of an hour before Ruslan''s lock finally cycled open, not hiding it, not pretending he''d wandered down for any other reason, just standing where the ramp met the passage floor with his arms crossed the same way he stood in every meeting, so that when Ruslan stepped through — alone this time, no Sigrun at his shoulder, no wall reading behind him for him to measure himself against — there wasn''t a version of the morning where the two of them didn''t have this conversation.

"Adeyinka," Wen said, which wasn''t quite a greeting.

"Castellanos." Ruslan set down the case he''d carried up with him, unhurried, the way a man sets something down when he already suspects the next few minutes aren''t going to be about the case. "You''ve been standing here a while."

"Long enough." Wen didn''t uncross his arms. "I read the salvage terms again last night. All of it, the parts I skipped the first time because I already knew what they said. There''s a line in section four — certified salvage, structural steel — that doesn''t specify. I want you to specify it to my face, since you''re the one who signed the walk-through with my shop''s fixtures on the inventory."

"Specify what, exactly."

"Whether the jigs get recycled or melted. I know those sound like the same word to a broker. They''re not the same word to me. Recycled, there''s a version where somebody, somewhere, uses the metal again as what it already is — a jig, a fixture, something with the shape my grandfather cut into it still meaning something to whoever gets it next. Melted, there isn''t. Melted, it''s just steel again, and it goes wherever steel goes, and the shape''s gone the second the furnace takes it. I want to know which one I''m losing, plainly, before I stand in that room tonight and cast a ballot I already know the direction of."

Ruslan didn''t look away from him, and didn''t reach for anything softer than what he had to say next.

"They''re the same process," Ruslan said. "There isn''t a version where your jigs come out the other side as jigs. Certified salvage means the metal''s reclaimed to spec and sold by weight to whoever''s buying structural steel that quarter. It goes into the same furnace as the framing members, the same furnace as every fixture on this bloc that isn''t personal effects. I could tell you there''s a recycling stream that keeps shapes intact for reuse, and there is one, for some materials, in some markets. It isn''t the stream your jigs go into. I''m not going to describe you a process that sounds gentler than the one that''s actually contracted, because you''d find out the difference eventually, and I''d rather you find it out from me, today, than from a strip crew in six weeks."

"That''s not an answer I wanted," Wen said.

"No," Ruslan agreed. "It''s the answer, though. I don''t have a version of it that changes shape depending on who''s asking, or how long they''ve owned the tools, or how many years their grandfather spent calibrating something I''ve never once seen run. I looked at your shop on the walk-through same as I looked at every fixture on this bloc, and I priced it the way the contract prices fixture mass, which is by weight, because that''s the only column the contract has for it. I know that''s not the column you''d have chosen for it. I don''t have a different one to offer you."

Wen was quiet for a moment, and the quiet had weight to it, the particular kind he recognized from thirty years of standing over a jig that had come back from calibration wrong and needing a second before he said anything he''d regret.

"Is it cruel," he asked finally, "telling me plainly instead of letting me keep some version of hope about it until the strip crew''s already got the lathes half apart?"

"I don''t think so," Ruslan said, "but I understand why you''d ask it. I''ve had that question put to me by three different blocs now, in three different rooms, and I''ve given the same answer every time, because I don''t think I''ve found a better one. Hope that doesn''t survive contact with the contract isn''t kindness. It''s just delay, and delay costs the person hoping more than it costs me. I''d rather be the man who told you honestly at the freight lock than the one who let you believe something gentler and watched you find out otherwise from a work order. That''s not cruelty. That''s just where I''ve landed on it, doing this as long as I have."

"You could be wrong about that," Wen said. "About where you''ve landed."

"I could be," Ruslan said, and something in his voice, for the first time since he''d stepped off the ramp, sounded less like a man reciting a position and more like a man who''d actually turned the question over more than once and hadn''t fully settled it even now. "I''ve thought about it more than you''d guess, standing on ramps like this one. I haven''t found the version of honesty that costs less than this one does, not for you, not for the shop, not for the three blocs before yours. If you find it, I''d want to hear it. I mean that. I''m not saying it to end the conversation."

Wen didn''t answer that. He looked past Ruslan, down the ramp toward the lock that had already begun cycling shut behind him, sealing the freight bay back into the bloc''s ordinary quiet, and found he had nothing left to ask that the man hadn''t already answered as plainly as he''d asked it.

"I''ll vote no tonight," he said. "You already knew that."

"I did," Ruslan said. "I didn''t come up here today to change it. I came up because Sigrun asked me to confirm the inventory figures one more time before the count, and because I thought, if I was going to be in the building anyway, you deserved the answer you just asked for, in person, instead of in a document you''d have had to read twice to be sure you understood it right the first time."

He picked the case back up, and neither of them said anything further, and Wen watched him go the long way down the passage toward the co-op office, understanding, in the particular vocabulary he''d spent thirty years building for exactly this kind of understanding, that he had just gotten the truest answer available to him, that the truth hadn''t moved him an inch, and that both of those things could be so at the same time without either one canceling the other out.' WHERE Id='019F3AC8-397D-7B63-8663-EC0908A8D08B';